#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d17bfb9a-8e07-559b-8fac-6af97f273834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358074} {:answer/id #uuid "43657de1-8aab-5d63-bb61-a029e159baa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}+yz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358075} {:answer/id #uuid "4111b42c-485d-50ef-8134-b04c0b205a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}z-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358076} {:answer/id #uuid "4083e5c0-fa10-5091-901b-60ddde4d0475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358077} {:answer/id #uuid "ae231568-11a6-5d3b-b716-61adf9c41929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz-y{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4083e5c0-fa10-5091-901b-60ddde4d0475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358077} :answer-field/id #uuid "27d0cb5f-d94b-5736-b8d7-1396257b6194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6dd231c-6e98-5338-9bc2-ef1a31fa9648" :question/math-academy-id "q-124733" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+(3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z})\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2xy{z}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) & =3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3406d2d3-08a2-58d6-a4ba-b0fcd245209f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358081} {:answer/id #uuid "ec60e2c0-ce80-5367-8b2b-a16c15b4acde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358082} {:answer/id #uuid "020e4eb5-f69a-52ce-a50d-34c8fbbce48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2y{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358083} {:answer/id #uuid "7c516d5a-291b-57d6-9746-a24d02de40e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358084} {:answer/id #uuid "59d31e27-0aa0-566f-ae20-67eb96b31839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c516d5a-291b-57d6-9746-a24d02de40e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2133f6a4-f86a-564d-86bb-b566df8c38be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56db4f7d-60e2-5a2d-b5c8-a70229b1b98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132080" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+2y)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3458a7e-ccf3-541d-bb91-4a16aa05357c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358088} {:answer/id #uuid "baf3329c-ec0b-5776-90be-611bc51dfed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358089} {:answer/id #uuid "943ef10b-a598-5663-a926-af18c265095b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358090} {:answer/id #uuid "084d6fb5-1145-5d40-8082-7323b55834ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-\\frac{x}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358091} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa9c242-dfa9-5339-a37d-3f3ad4778e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{3}y-\\frac{x}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3458a7e-ccf3-541d-bb91-4a16aa05357c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358088} :answer-field/id #uuid "f687fd7b-0748-53fe-89b3-940ba8bde874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4cd2d9c-aa9c-533b-b610-fc91804dad9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81737" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,i+(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ g(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=\\frac{1}{y}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5ed65ed-d417-5fa7-b74a-9d69f889aad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=(x+y){e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358095} {:answer/id #uuid "54f8a568-c21f-5a39-b16a-2db3381ef317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358096} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba7c4f6-95a4-54f4-839c-ab7995e838c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358097} {:answer/id #uuid "1af6b8fa-ad47-5b32-81c5-408484fa026d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358098} {:answer/id #uuid "26bb2150-bd4d-5ee4-a519-ae7cf9d85f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54f8a568-c21f-5a39-b16a-2db3381ef317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358096} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e509f71-ed01-57c1-9d73-52287c9b0ee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "def26eac-f7dd-5378-8a75-b8b2f9ba1f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-81741" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(z) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z) \\\\ h(z) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56953072-83dd-53c5-bd82-21711aa98f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358102} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8db0b3-97b6-51d8-b18e-4053a330473d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358103} {:answer/id #uuid "658ec369-19b5-52ad-b51f-1b14f85d8320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=xy{e}^{xy}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358104} {:answer/id #uuid "66bd6623-0740-536c-941c-e7c3aa1f86ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}+2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358105} {:answer/id #uuid "68ecce04-ff69-553c-a616-7a58a97ee24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba8db0b3-97b6-51d8-b18e-4053a330473d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358103} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5754676-fd25-51bd-89a3-fabf150ef837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "569f8015-7dc4-5a18-8474-832c2e68650b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81749" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{xy}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"}]}
